<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch07_P06_Ex1_Faded_Step</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The exercise above asks you to prove that \(1^3 + 2^3 + \cdots + n^3 = \left(\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\right)^2\) for \(n \geq 1\). Here is its induction step with some parts missing.</p>
<p>Assume that the statement holds for \(n = k\):</p>
<p>\[1^3 + 2^3 + \cdots + k^3 = \left(\dfrac{k(k+1)}{2}\right)^2.\]</p>
<p>Then</p>
<div>\(1^3 + 2^3 + \cdots + k^3 + (k+1)^3 =\) [[input:ans1]] \(+\ (k+1)^3\), because of [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans1]] [[validation:ans4]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>
<div>\(= \dfrac{(k+1)^2}{4} \times \Big(\) [[input:ans2]] \(\Big)\) [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<div>\(= \dfrac{(k+1)^2 (k+2)^2}{4}\)</div>
<div>\(= \Big(\) [[input:ans3]] \(\Big)^2,\) [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>which is the statement for \(n = k+1\).</p>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The induction step starts from the left-hand side of the statement for \(n = k+1\) and uses the induction hypothesis to replace the first \(k\) terms. Each line has one reason.</p>
<p>\[1^3 + 2^3 + \cdots + k^3 + (k+1)^3 = \dfrac{k^2(k+1)^2}{4} + (k+1)^3\]</p>
<p>by the induction hypothesis. Taking out the common factor \(\dfrac{(k+1)^2}{4}\),</p>
<p>\[= \dfrac{(k+1)^2}{4}\left(k^2 + 4(k+1)\right) = \dfrac{(k+1)^2}{4}\left(k^2 + 4k + 4\right).\]</p>
<p>Since \(k^2 + 4k + 4 = (k+2)^2\) and \(4 = 2^2\),</p>
<p>\[= \dfrac{(k+1)^2 (k+2)^2}{4} = \left(\dfrac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}\right)^2.\]</p>
<p>So the blanks are \(\dfrac{k^2(k+1)^2}{4}\) (by the induction hypothesis), \(k^2 + 4k + 4\) and \(\dfrac{(k+1)(k+2)}{2}\). The last line is \(\left(\dfrac{(k+1)\big((k+1)+1\big)}{2}\right)^2\), which is the statement for \(n = k+1\).</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
